can mention Sleeping Beauty briefly? Could include but not necessary? The prompt says apply EDT to decision problems, central definitions. Maybe mention Sleeping Beauty as open application with uncertainty; but could be tangential. Maybe include a paragraph on \"open questions\" including Sleeping Beauty and conditional probability zero. Need source leaves important question open: how conditional probabilities for own acts? Need focus on that. Could mention Sleeping Beauty: source says EDT applied to avoid Dutch books but Conitzer argues halfers still affected. We can say not central; indexical uncertainty complicates. But not necessary? Maybe include to show source open? However prompt asks where source leaves important question open. The source doesn't explain how to operationalize conditionals for acts with probability zero or policy evaluation. That is better.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"news value\" and \"do what you most want to learn that you will do.\" Good quote. Need explain.\n\nNeed write as mathematical essay. Use LaTeX style. Final answer should be text only with formulas. Need perhaps mention source claims with phrases \"According to the reference article...\" and \"I add...\".\n\nNeed consider if formula uses D(O_j) desirability. Need maybe define D vs U. Use V for EDT. CDT uses U maybe. Need avoid confusion with utility function. Use D for desirability.\n\nNeed maybe discuss decision under acts with non-zero probability. In Bayesian decision theory, acts often chosen, not probabilistic; EDT conditioning on act event is unusual because acts are not states. Need address.\n\nLet's deep dive into EDT theory for precision.\n\nEvidential decision theory: associated with Jeffrey (1965?) and later. It treats acts as propositions. The desirability of a proposition is expected utility conditional on it. For act A, V(A)=∑_j P(O_j|A)D(O_j). Choose act maximizing. In Jeffrey's framework, desires are assigned to propositions, not states. The source uses Gibbard & Harp